   <text>
      <body>
         <div type="article">
            <head>
               <title type="main">From Railway Station to Museum: The Story of the Musée d’Orsay</title>
               <title type="sub">A Case of Cultural Heritage Transformation</title>
            </head>

            <p>
               This project focuses on the transformation of the
               <placeName ref="#gareOrsay">Gare d’Orsay</placeName>
               from a railway station into one of the most important art museums in the world.
               The <placeName ref="#gareOrsay">Gare d’Orsay</placeName>
               was originally built between
               <date when="1898">1898</date>
               and
               <date when="1900">1900</date>
               in <placeName ref="#paris">Paris</placeName>
               and was designed mainly by the architect
               <persName ref="#victorLaloux">Victor Laloux</persName>.
               It officially opened on
               <date when="1900-05-28">28 May 1900</date>,
               in time for the
               <rs type="event" ref="#expo1900">Exposition Universelle of 1900</rs>.
               At that time, the building represented both technological progress and architectural
               elegance: it was a modern railway terminal, but its exterior was designed in the
               monumental <term ref="#beauxArtsStyle">Beaux-Arts style</term>.
            </p>

            <p>
               By <date when="1939">1939</date>, the station had become less suitable for modern
               railway traffic because its platforms were too short for longer trains. As a result,
               the building gradually lost its original function. During the following decades, it was
               used for different purposes and was even threatened with demolition.
            </p>

            <p>
               In the <date when="1970">1970s</date>, a new idea appeared: instead of destroying
               the former station, it could be preserved and transformed into a museum. This decision
               was important because it showed a new attitude toward historical industrial buildings.
               The <placeName ref="#gareOrsay">Gare d’Orsay</placeName>
               was no longer seen only as an obsolete railway structure, but as a valuable piece of
               architectural and <term ref="#culturalHeritage">cultural heritage</term>.
               The future museum was also intended to fill the gap between the collections of the
               <placeName ref="#louvre">Louvre</placeName>
               and the
               <placeName ref="#nationalMuseumModernArt">National Museum of Modern Art</placeName>
               at the <placeName ref="#centrePompidou">Centre Pompidou</placeName>.
            </p>

            <p>
               The transformation project was supported by French political figures including
               <persName ref="#georgesPompidou">Georges Pompidou</persName>
               and
               <persName ref="#valeryGiscard">Valéry Giscard d’Estaing</persName>.
               In <date when="1978">1978</date>, an
               <rs type="event" ref="#architecturalCompetition1978">architectural competition</rs>
               was organised, and the project was awarded to the
               <orgName ref="#actArchitectureGroup">ACT architecture group</orgName>,
               led by <persName ref="#pierreColboc">Pierre Colboc</persName>.
               The Italian architect and designer
               <persName ref="#gaeAulenti">Gae Aulenti</persName>
               was responsible for the interior design. Her work adapted the vast railway hall into
               a museum space while preserving the memory of the original station.
            </p>

            <p>
               The building reopened in
               <date when="1986-12">December 1986</date>
               as the <placeName ref="#museeOrsay">Musée d’Orsay</placeName>.
               Its new identity combined the old and the new: the former train shed became a grand
               exhibition hall, while the former railway hotel was adapted into gallery spaces.
               The result was not simply a museum placed inside an old station, but a cultural
               institution whose architecture became part of the visitor experience.
            </p>

            <p>
               Today, the <placeName ref="#museeOrsay">Musée d’Orsay</placeName>
               is internationally known for its collections of French art from the period
               <date from="1848" to="1914">1848–1914</date>.
               It holds paintings, sculptures, furniture, photography, and decorative arts.
               It is especially famous for its
               <term ref="#impressionism">Impressionist</term>
               and
               <term ref="#postImpressionism">Post-Impressionist</term>
               masterpieces, including works by
               <persName ref="#claudeMonet">Claude Monet</persName>,
               <persName ref="#paulGauguin">Paul Gauguin</persName>,
               <persName ref="#vincentVanGogh">Vincent van Gogh</persName>,
               <persName ref="#edouardManet">Édouard Manet</persName>,
               <persName ref="#edgarDegas">Edgar Degas</persName>,
               <persName ref="#pierreAugusteRenoir">Pierre-Auguste Renoir</persName>,
               and <persName ref="#paulCezanne">Paul Cézanne</persName>.
            </p>

            <p>
               The building is not only a museum but also an important cultural symbol. Its unique
               atmosphere has also inspired films, such as the documentary
               <rs type="documentary" ref="#oneNightOrsayMuseum">One Night in Orsay Museum</rs>
               by <persName ref="#olivierLemaire">Olivier Lemaire</persName>,
               which shows the museum at night and reflects on its transformation
               <quote>from a railway station into a space dedicated to art</quote>.
               The museum is also accessible through digital cultural resources, including the
               <rs type="digitalResource" ref="#googleVirtualTour">virtual tour of the Musée d’Orsay</rs>
               provided by
               <orgName ref="#googleArtsCulture">Google Arts &amp; Culture</orgName>,
               which allows visitors to explore the museum space online.
            </p>

            <p>
               This project is therefore about the transformation of a building and its meaning.
               The <placeName ref="#gareOrsay">Gare d’Orsay</placeName>
               moved from transport to culture, from industrial modernity to museum heritage,
               and from threatened demolition to preservation. This makes it a case of
               <term ref="#culturalHeritageTransformation">cultural heritage transformation</term>
               and also an example of <term ref="#adaptiveReuse">adaptive reuse</term>.
            </p>

            <p>
               This reading is echoed internationally: the
               <rs type="reference" ref="#icamtAdaptiveReuseArticle">Adaptive Reuse</rs>
               reference page published by
               <orgName ref="#icamt">ARCHI-TECHS</orgName>, ICOM’s International Committee
               for Architecture and Museum Techniques, names the Musée d’Orsay a
               <quote>successful and well-known case of adaptive reuse</quote>,
               pointing to the surviving ornate gold clock above the former tracks as a
               visible trace of the building’s history as the Gare d’Orsay.
            </p>

            <p>
               The concept is also documented more broadly on
               <rs type="reference" ref="#wikipediaAdaptiveReuse">Wikipedia’s Adaptive reuse
               entry</rs>, published by the
               <orgName ref="#wikimediaFoundation">Wikimedia Foundation</orgName>, which frames
               adaptive reuse as an architectural and urban-planning strategy applicable well
               beyond the museum sector.
            </p>

            <p>
               The theoretical framing draws further on
               <title level="m" ref="#adaptiveReuseBuiltHeritageBook">Adaptive Reuse of the Built
               Heritage: Concepts and Cases of an Emerging Discipline</title> by
               <persName ref="#biePlevoets">Bie Plevoets</persName> and
               <persName ref="#koenraadVanCleempoel">Koenraad Van Cleempoel</persName>, which
               establishes adaptive reuse as an emerging design discipline through theoretical
               concepts and international case studies.
            </p>
         </div>
      </body>
   </text>

